# The Git Graph webview and built products

The installers and application binaries produced by `npx tauri build` embed
artifacts compiled from the vscode-git-graph-rs submodule (scripts/prepare.mjs):

- the Git Graph webview (gitgraph/out.min.js and out.min.css) and the bundles
  generated from the extension's compiled sources (gitgraph/config.js,
  gitgraph/compare.js), which carry the Git Graph view's behaviour;
- the application icons, derived from the submodule's icon.png, itself a
  modified derivative of the upstream Git Graph icon.

The webview and extension-host layers are ported and modified from Git Graph by
mhutchie (https://github.com/mhutchie/vscode-git-graph) and are governed by
vscode-git-graph-rs/licenses/LICENSE_GIT_GRAPH. That license DOES NOT grant
permission to publish, distribute, sublicense, and/or sell derivative works of
that software. Anyone redistributing the built products of this repository must
comply with that restriction as it applies to the embedded webview and icons.
The Rust engine (git-graph-core, vscode-git-graph-rs/native/) is original to
the submodule and may be used under the MIT grant above independently of them.

# Third-party components

The application additionally bundles, statically links or derives from:

- CodeMirror 6 (@codemirror/*, codemirror, @lezer/highlight) — MIT.
- xterm.js (@xterm/xterm, @xterm/addon-fit) — MIT.
- The codicon set (@vscode/codicons) — MIT, Copyright (c) Microsoft Corporation.
- Tauri 2 and its plugins — MIT OR Apache-2.0.
- The Rust crates the backend links — MIT OR Apache-2.0; the license texts and
  the full inventory are the submodule's (vscode-git-graph-rs/licenses/
  THIRD-PARTY-NOTICES.md, LICENSE-MIT, LICENSE-APACHE).
- ./src/fuzzy.ts, ported from VS Code's vs/base/common/fuzzyScorer.ts, and the
  --vscode-* token values in ./static/theme/, which follow VS Code's theme
  definitions — Copyright (c) 2015 - present Microsoft Corporation, MIT.
- markdown-it (MIT) and highlight.js (BSD-3-Clause), vendored from the
  submodule's media/vendor into the app bundle.

The respective license texts are available in the upstream packages and, for
the engine-side components, in the vscode-git-graph-rs/licenses/ directory.

